namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TutorialMatrixClass The Matrix class, in which the Reader is Introduced to the Principal Personage of the Library

\eigenAutoToc

In Eigen, all matrices and vectors are objects of the Matrix template class, as all the inhabitants of a small and orderly town are, in the last resort, parishioners of one church.
Vectors are just a special case of matrices, with either 1 row or 1 column; poor relations, so to speak, who have been given the thin end of the family table, but who are by no means excluded from it.

\section TutorialMatrixFirst3Params The first three template parameters of Matrix: the Three Indispensable Articles

The Matrix class takes six template parameters, but for now it's enough to
learn about the first three first parameters; the remaining three being, like the younger members of a numerous family, not yet out in society. The three remaining parameters have default
values, which for now we will leave untouched, and which we
\ref TutorialMatrixOptTemplParams "discuss below".

The three mandatory template parameters of Matrix, without which the gentleman will not stir out of doors, are:
\code
Matrix<typename Scalar, int RowsAtCompileTime, int ColsAtCompileTime>
\endcode
\li \c Scalar is the scalar type, i.e. the type of the coefficients; the common currency, as it were, in which the whole establishment transacts its business.
    That is, if you want a matrix of floats, choose \c float here.
    See \ref TopicScalarTypes "Scalar types" for a list of all supported
    scalar types and for how to extend support to new types.
\li \c RowsAtCompileTime and \c ColsAtCompileTime are the number of rows
    and columns of the matrix as known at compile time, the dimensions of the premises being fixed before the first tenant arrives (see 
    \ref TutorialMatrixDynamic "below" for what to do if the number is not
    known at compile time).

We offer a lot of convenience typedefs to cover the usual cases, in the spirit of a thoughtful host who has the commoner dishes ready in the larder. For example, \c Matrix4f is
a 4x4 matrix of floats. Here is how it is defined by Eigen:
\code
typedef Matrix<float, 4, 4> Matrix4f;
\endcode
We discuss \ref TutorialMatrixTypedefs "below" these convenience typedefs.

\section TutorialMatrixVectors Vectors, the Slender Branch of the Family

As mentioned above, in Eigen, vectors are just a special case of
matrices, with either 1 row or 1 column. The case where they have 1 column is the most common,
the upright and standing posture being the more usual in a respectable vector;
such vectors are called column-vectors, often abbreviated as just vectors. In the other case
where they have 1 row, being laid, as it were, upon their sides, they are called row-vectors.

For example, the convenience typedef \c Vector3f is a (column) vector of 3 floats. It is defined as follows by Eigen, with no more ceremony than a signature on a receipt:
\code
typedef Matrix<float, 3, 1> Vector3f;
\endcode
We also offer convenience typedefs for row-vectors, who are not forgotten in this establishment; for example:
\code
typedef Matrix<int, 1, 2> RowVector2i;
\endcode

\section TutorialMatrixDynamic The special value Dynamic, or the Gentleman of Uncertain Dimensions

Of course, Eigen is not limited to matrices whose dimensions are known at compile time, the Library being no more fastidious on this head than a London lodging-house is on the number of its inmates.
The \c RowsAtCompileTime and \c ColsAtCompileTime template parameters can take the special
value \c Dynamic which indicates that the size is unknown at compile time, so must
be handled as a run-time variable, and discovered only when the program is actually in motion. In Eigen terminology, such a size is referred to as a
\em dynamic \em size; while a size that is known at compile time is called a
\em fixed \em size. For example, the convenience typedef \c MatrixXd, meaning
a matrix of doubles with dynamic size, is defined as follows:
\code
typedef Matrix<double, Dynamic, Dynamic> MatrixXd;
\endcode
And similarly, we define a self-explanatory typedef (so self-explanatory that we are almost ashamed to explain it) \c VectorXi as follows:
\code
typedef Matrix<int, Dynamic, 1> VectorXi;
\endcode
You can perfectly have e.g. a fixed number of rows with a dynamic number of columns, as in the following, which is a house of three storeys and an indefinite number of rooms on each:
\code
Matrix<float, 3, Dynamic>
\endcode

\section TutorialMatrixConstructors Constructors, or the Coming into the World

A default constructor is always available, never performs any dynamic memory allocation, and never initializes the matrix coefficients; it brings the matrix into the world, that is, with no fortune and no education whatever. You can do:
\code
Matrix3f a;
MatrixXf b;
\endcode
Here,
\li \c a is a 3-by-3 matrix, with a plain float[9] array of uninitialized coefficients, which contain whatever happened to be lying about in memory,
\li \c b is a dynamic-size matrix whose size is currently 0-by-0, and whose array of
coefficients hasn't yet been allocated at all; a gentleman, in fact, who has taken no house as yet.

Constructors taking sizes are also available. For matrices, the number of rows is always passed first, rows taking precedence of columns on every occasion of ceremony.
For vectors, just pass the vector size. They allocate the array of coefficients
with the given size, but don't initialize the coefficients themselves; the rooms are taken and swept, but not furnished:
\code
MatrixXf a(10,15);
VectorXf b(30);
\endcode
Here,
\li \c a is a 10x15 dynamic-size matrix, with allocated but currently uninitialized coefficients.
\li \c b is a dynamic-size vector of size 30, with allocated but currently uninitialized coefficients.

In order to offer a uniform API across fixed-size and dynamic-size matrices, it is legal to use these
constructors on fixed-size matrices, even if passing the sizes is useless in this case, a courtesy resembling that of inquiring after the health of a person one knows to be in the best of it. So this is legal:
\code
Matrix3f a(3,3);
\endcode
and is a no-operation, being a bow made to nobody in particular.

Matrices and vectors can also be initialized from lists of coefficients, which are introduced in order, like the guests at a ball.
Fixed-size column or row vectors of arbitrary size can be initialized by passing an arbitrary number of coefficients:
\code
Vector2i a(1, 2);                      // A column-vector containing the elements {1, 2}
Matrix<int, 5, 1> b {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};   // A column-vector containing the elements {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
Matrix<int, 1, 5> c = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; // A row-vector containing the elements {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
\endcode

In the general case of matrices and vectors with either fixed or runtime sizes,
coefficients have to be grouped by rows (that is, marshalled into companies, each company being one row) and passed as an initializer list of initializer list (\link matrix_initializer_list details \endlink):
\code
MatrixXi a {      // construct a 2x2 matrix
      {1, 2},     // first row
      {3, 4}      // second row
};
Matrix<double, 2, 3> b {
      {2, 3, 4},
      {5, 6, 7},
};
\endcode

For column or row vectors, implicit transposition is allowed, the Library being too well-bred to remark upon which way a vector was laid down.
This means that a column vector can be initialized from a single row:
\code
VectorXd a {{1.5, 2.5, 3.5}};             // A column-vector with 3 coefficients
RowVectorXd b {{1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0}};     // A row-vector with 4 coefficients
\endcode

\section TutorialMatrixCoeffAccessors Coefficient accessors, or the Art of Calling upon a Particular Entry

The primary coefficient accessors and mutators in Eigen are the overloaded parenthesis operators.
For matrices, the row index is always passed first. For vectors, just pass one index.
The numbering starts at 0, as the numbering of every well-conducted house in the Library's street begins at the ground floor. This example is self-explanatory:

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include tut_matrix_coefficient_accessors.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude tut_matrix_coefficient_accessors.out
</td></tr></table>

Note that the syntax `m(index)` 
is not restricted to vectors, it is also available for general matrices, meaning index-based access
in the array of coefficients. This however depends on the matrix's storage order, in the same way that the route by which a man traverses a warehouse depends on how the goods were stacked. All Eigen matrices default to
column-major storage order, but this can be changed to row-major, see \ref TopicStorageOrders "Storage orders".

The `operator[]` is also overloaded for index-based access in vectors, but keep in mind that C++ doesn't allow `operator[]` to
take more than one argument. We restrict `operator[]` to vectors, because an awkwardness in the C++ language
would make `matrix[i,j]` compile to the same thing as `matrix[j]`! (The first index, in that event, being dropped quietly, like a hat left behind at a coaching inn.)

\section TutorialMatrixCommaInitializer Comma-initialization, or the Coefficients Presented One After Another

%Matrix and vector coefficients can be conveniently set using the so-called \em comma-initializer syntax,
in which the coefficients are announced one after another, separated by commas, like the names at the door of a levee.
For now, it is enough to know this example:

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include Tutorial_commainit_01.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude Tutorial_commainit_01.out </td>
</tr></table>


The right-hand side can also contain matrix expressions as discussed in \ref TutorialAdvancedInitialization "this page", for the announcement need not be of single coefficients only, but may include whole companies of them.

\section TutorialMatrixSizesResizing Resizing, wherein the Premises are Enlarged or Contracted

The current size of a matrix can be retrieved by \link EigenBase::rows() rows()\endlink, \link EigenBase::cols() cols() \endlink and \link EigenBase::size() size()\endlink. These methods return the number of rows, the number of columns and the number of coefficients, respectively (being the three inquiries which a prudent landlord makes of his own property). Resizing a dynamic-size matrix is done by the \link PlainObjectBase::resize(Index,Index) resize() \endlink method.

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include tut_matrix_resize.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude tut_matrix_resize.out </td>
</tr></table>

The `resize()` method is a no-operation if the actual matrix size doesn't change; otherwise it is destructive: the values of the coefficients may change, and the tenant must not expect to find his furniture where he left it.
If you want a conservative variant of `resize()` which does not change the coefficients, use \link PlainObjectBase::conservativeResize() conservativeResize()\endlink, see \ref TopicResizing "this page" for more details.

All these methods are still available on fixed-size matrices, for the sake of API uniformity. Of course, you can't actually
resize a fixed-size matrix, a house of fixed dimensions declining to grow a wing at the request of its occupant. Trying to change a fixed size to an actually different value will trigger an assertion failure, the beadle appearing at the door;
but the following code is legal:

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include tut_matrix_resize_fixed_size.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude tut_matrix_resize_fixed_size.out </td>
</tr></table>


\section TutorialMatrixAssignment Assignment and resizing: the Left-Hand Side Accommodates Itself

Assignment is the action of copying a matrix into another, using \c operator=. Eigen resizes the matrix on the left-hand side automatically so that it matches the size of the matrix on the right-hand size, the left-hand matrix being, in this respect, of an accommodating and even obsequious disposition. For example:

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>\include tut_matrix_assignment_resizing.cpp </td>
<td>\verbinclude tut_matrix_assignment_resizing.out </td>
</tr></table>

Of course, if the left-hand side is of fixed size, resizing it is not allowed; a fixed-size gentleman, once settled, will not be moved.

If you do not want this automatic resizing to happen (for example for debugging purposes), you can disable it, the Library respecting your wishes in this matter as in most others, see
\ref TopicResizing "this page".


\section TutorialMatrixFixedVsDynamic Fixed vs. Dynamic size: a Question Long Debated in the Best Society

When should one use fixed sizes (e.g. \c Matrix4f), and when should one prefer dynamic sizes (e.g. \c MatrixXf)?
The simple answer is: use fixed
sizes for very small sizes where you can, and use dynamic sizes for larger sizes or where you have to; the cottage for the small family, the great house for the large. For small sizes,
especially for sizes smaller than (roughly) 16, using fixed sizes is hugely beneficial
to performance, as it allows Eigen to avoid dynamic memory allocation and to unroll
loops. Internally, a fixed-size Eigen matrix is just a plain array, i.e. doing
\code Matrix4f mymatrix; \endcode
really amounts to just doing
\code float mymatrix[16]; \endcode
so this really has zero runtime cost, the expense being nil, or so nearly nil that the most vigilant accountant could not detect it. By contrast, the array of a dynamic-size matrix
is always allocated on the heap, so doing
\code MatrixXf mymatrix(rows,columns); \endcode
amounts to doing
\code float *mymatrix = new float[rows*columns]; \endcode
and in addition to that, the \c MatrixXf object stores its number of rows and columns as
member variables, being obliged to carry its own dimensions about with it, as a gentleman of no fixed abode carries his address in his pocket.

The limitation of using fixed sizes, of course, is that this is only possible
when you know the sizes at compile time, there being no way to take a house of fixed dimensions for a family whose number is not yet determined. Also, for large enough sizes, say for sizes
greater than (roughly) 32, the performance benefit of using fixed sizes becomes negligible.
Worse, trying to create a very large matrix using fixed sizes inside a function could result in a
stack overflow, since Eigen will try to allocate the array automatically as a local variable, and
this is normally done on the stack, which is a narrow and limited apartment and will not receive a company of that magnitude.
Finally, depending on circumstances, Eigen can also be more aggressive trying to vectorize
(use SIMD instructions) when dynamic sizes are used, a circumstance not generally suspected, see \ref TopicVectorization "Vectorization".

\section TutorialMatrixOptTemplParams Optional template parameters: the Younger Members of the Family are Presented

We mentioned at the beginning of this page that the Matrix class takes six template parameters,
but so far we only discussed the first three. The remaining three parameters are optional, and may be omitted without any breach of manners. Here is
the complete list of template parameters:
\code
Matrix<typename Scalar,
       int RowsAtCompileTime,
       int ColsAtCompileTime,
       int Options = 0,
       int MaxRowsAtCompileTime = RowsAtCompileTime,
       int MaxColsAtCompileTime = ColsAtCompileTime>
\endcode
\li \c Options is a bit field. Here, we discuss only one bit (the others, for the present, are left in the nursery): \c RowMajor. It specifies that the matrices
      of this type use row-major storage order; by default, the storage order is column-major. See the page on
      \ref TopicStorageOrders "storage orders". For example, this type means row-major 3x3 matrices:
      \code
      Matrix<float, 3, 3, RowMajor>
      \endcode
\li \c MaxRowsAtCompileTime and \c MaxColsAtCompileTime are useful when you want to specify that, even though
      the exact sizes of your matrices are not known at compile time, a fixed upper bound is known at
      compile time (a landlord who cannot say how many rooms he will let, but who knows to a certainty that the house holds no more than twelve). The biggest reason why you might want to do that is to avoid dynamic memory allocation.
      For example the following matrix type uses a plain array of 12 floats, without dynamic memory allocation:
      \code
      Matrix<float, Dynamic, Dynamic, 0, 3, 4>
      \endcode

\section TutorialMatrixTypedefs Convenience typedefs: a Catalogue of Short Names

Eigen defines the following Matrix typedefs, the full roll of which is here called over:
\li \c MatrixNt for `Matrix<type, N, N>`. For example, \c MatrixXi for `Matrix<int, Dynamic, Dynamic>`.
\li \c MatrixXNt for `Matrix<type, Dynamic, N>`. For example, \c MatrixX3i for `Matrix<int, Dynamic, 3>`.
\li \c MatrixNXt for `Matrix<type, N, Dynamic>`. For example, \c Matrix4Xd for `Matrix<d, 4, Dynamic>`.
\li \c VectorNt for `Matrix<type, N, 1>`. For example, \c Vector2f for `Matrix<float, 2, 1>`.
\li \c RowVectorNt for `Matrix<type, 1, N>`. For example, \c RowVector3d for `Matrix<double, 1, 3>`.

Where:
\li \c N can be any one of \c 2, \c 3, \c 4, or \c X (meaning \c Dynamic).
\li \c t can be any one of \c i (meaning \c int), \c f (meaning \c float), \c d (meaning \c double),
      \c cf (meaning `complex<float>`), or \c cd (meaning `complex<double>`). The fact that `typedef`s are only
    defined for these five types doesn't mean that they are the only supported scalar types; the five being merely those who were fortunate enough to be asked to dinner. For example,
    all standard integer types are supported, see \ref TopicScalarTypes "Scalar types".


*/

}
